residents in gaza and israel are marking  the first day of peace after an 11-day  conflict that killed over 230 people in  gaza  and 12 in israel the ceasefire deal  brokered mostly by egypt  carries no preconditions and leaves the  conflict's key disputes  unresolved world leaders and diplomats  are now pushing for a long-term  political solution but today  residents are picking up the pieces  after the region's  worst conflict in years  the streets of gaza have come back to  life  the ceasefire has brought relief but  people here  know just how fragile peace is  life will return because this is not the  first war  and it will not be the last one normal  life will return  it is true that our hearts are in pain  there were disasters  entire families were wiped out and that  saddens us  but it is our fate in this land to be  patient  the truce was welcomed in jerusalem  where tensions flared for weeks before  fighting began it has to be done  to be honest with you because of the  bloodshed that  and the murdered and the children  that was killed that were killed and the  innocent women  everyone every soul has a value  so this has to be done but in this  israeli border town  opinions were mixed  zipped bad this is not a cease-fire  they should not have stopped what will  happen in a year  in half a year two years so  finally there is a cease-fire  both israel and hamas are claiming  victory in the conflict  the task of rebuilding now begins with  hopes  the truce will last  let's go straight to tanya krema that's  dw's  correspondent in gaza city tanya  is the ceasefire holding  well at the moment at least uh the  ceasefire seems to be holding we're in  gaza city of course that's also  on the minds of many people they're  worried about you know what could happen  if it doesn't hold so  at the moment uh it is quite in gaza but  it's a tense quiet  hamas of course portraying this as a  victory  will the ordinary people there in gaza  see it the same way  well i think it's a bit of a strange  atmosphere on the one hand of course  hamas and other militant groups portray  this as a victory but people here  now are coming out and after 11 days of  fighting of bombardment and  rocket strikes into israel and they have  to pick up their lies where they left it  11 days ago so it is not an easy  atmosphere at all  the devastation you can see it in gaza  many thousands tens of thousands of  people have  actually um lost have been displaced  so we saw some people you know slowly  going back and seeing  what is left of their home so it is not  an easy atmosphere and of course uh  there's also worry about whether the  ceasefire will hold  quite a lot for people to take in uh  how long will it be uh for gaza to  recover from all of this devastation  well it's probably hard to estimate  because the situation of gaza is not  easy it has been under a blockade for  almost  14 years that means every building  material  is it's not easy to bring it in either  from israel  or from egypt so uh we know that several  thousand  units at least have been destroyed or  severely uh damaged for people to live  in  so this will take time to rebuild of  course but i think at the moment the  focus is on  uh you know for those who have lost  loved ones you know it's a morning  period for those  who are injured to take care of them and  also for  you know the ordinary people here  somehow to pick up their lives  and that's as they say it's going to be  a difficult  uh situation for these people to resume  their lives are we seeing any  any support or anything of the scent of  the sort that will  come in if we get a sense of that  well several countries have already  pledged humanitarian aid  and there was also today because foreign  journalists like  us were led into gaza another crossing  with israel the karim shalom crossing  also  as far as we know opened for some  humanitarian aid coming in  egypt has also pledged to help there's  another crossing with egypt and the  south of gaza  so i think the international community  has also pledged to help rebuilding  but of course uh this is something  that's in the future so  um people you know for the moment  they're here and  they have to deal with what is there  here on the ground  okay dw's tanya crema in gaza city  thank you for bringing us up to speed  now israeli prime minister benjamin  netanyahu has hailed  the gaza offensive as an exceptional  success  and he warned hamas that there would be  a new level of force  should it resume rocket attacks on  israel now earlier today  netanyahu described what the offensive  had accomplished  we took out many targets in what they  call the metro below the gaza strip  we struck more than 100 kilometers of  that system  and we demonstrated that it was  vulnerable and could be destroyed  we eliminated more than 200 terrorists  among them 25  senior leaders including brigade  commanders  we eliminated an important part of the  hamas and islamic jihad leadership  and those who were not eliminated now  know that our long arm can reach them  anywhere  is a freelance journalist in jerusalem  and he joins us now sami welcome to the  program  are there any concerns about whether the  ceasefire will hold  yeah well i can tell you that here in  jerusalem  at the time that the palestinians have  been celebrating  what they see as a victory by the  palestinian  resistance israelis here  have had been in a different mood  but if we are just looking at the  question of whether the  ceasefire will continue or not it is  very clear that  the factions in the gaza strip are not  interested in resuming this round  and they have expressed uh just the  minutes ago again thanking the egyptians  for the role that they have  played even the events that we have seen  around the elector mosque  today uh the latest announcement has  been that  israel has withdrawn its troops from the  al-aqsa mosque so they don't see it as a  reason for a new  flare-up and what israelis have been  saying is that if there will be launches  from the gaza strip  then they will see it as a violation so  that means even if there will be a  balloon or something will be launched  from the gaza strip  there will be an israeli response so i  think one has to sometimes  look at what will happen along the  border with gaza  to see whether there will be such a  flare-up right and and what's the  situation in jerusalem looking like  right now  yeah well the situation was  after a night in which there were  thousands out in the streets  celebrating in east jerusalem and in  other  towns in the west bank there was the  friday a prayer and  i think some of the worshipers were  surprised to see  a police force in the compound of the  al-aqsa mosque  as you know the palestinian resistance  groups hamas and jihad as they refer to  themselves the palestinian resistance  they have claimed a big victory over  israel and they  talked about the fact that al-aqsa is  now has been liberated  so there was some attacks against the  policemen and the policemen responded  there were clashes  over 15 palestinians have been wounded  in these  clashes but it  seemed to be contained the situation  there has seemed to be a calm down  right um there's a report that in sheikh  there  uh israeli police are encircling the  area but  nevertheless we don't see a new round  it seems that it's calming down now  okay so nothing to threaten uh the  ceasefire at the moment  now uh a senior hamas leader ismail  haniye has been speaking  about the ceasefire can you tell us more  about what he said  so you know ismail aniye he was  thanking those who supported hamas  he was thanking iran for its  support both the military and  technological  support he was also thanking the united  nations  and egypt for their efforts to bring  about this  ceasefire and he was saying that  this has been a turning point in the  palestinian  struggle this is the narrative that that  you hear  and not only among hamas supporters but  they see this  event as being a turning point in the  sense that it has united palestinians  not only from the gaza strip but also  from the west bank  east jerusalem and even palestinians  with inside israel citizens of israel  who have been engaged during these  last few days and have expressed  solidarity  and went on strike the other day so all  of this is seen  as a victory for the resistance and  a defeat for abu mazen and mahmoud abbas  policies all right okay sami sokol in  jerusalem  thank you for bringing us up to speed
 

